    "available_at": "2026-11-16T23:10:07.166023+00:00",
    "body": "Asante Gold employees at a mine in Ghana. Credit: Asante Gold.\nPosted By: Northern Miner Staff November 16, 2026\nAsante Gold (TSXV: ASE; GSE: ASG) is examining whether an expanded open pit at the Bibiani mine in Ghana could defer underground development spending, as the miner works to recover from operating setbacks that forced it to withdraw this year’s guidance. The stock plunged for a second straight day.\nDrilling in the Bridge area between the Main Pit and the adjacent Cut 5 pit cut 60 metres at 3.13 grams gold per tonne from 103 metres depth. Another hole cut 6 metres at 62.48 grams from 136 metres, Asante said Monday .\n“Alongside the remaining drilling and test work, we are now prioritizing how best to mine this mineralization,” chief development officer Glenn Baldwin said.\nThe results offer a possible route to postpone spending scheduled for 2027–28, although revised economics are still being assessed. Moving material from an underground plan into a larger open pit would change how Asante accesses the deposit; it would not by itself resolve the mine’s current production problems.\nAsante shares, which had dropped 34% in Toronto Friday due to the profit warning, plunged a further 45% Monday morning to 23¢ to leave the company with a $210 million (US$143 million) valuation. They have lost 87% of their value since Jan. 1.\nGuidance withdrawn\nAsante on Friday withdrew its 2026 production and cost forecasts , citing contractor-equipment shortages, weaker plant recoveries, power interruptions and slower resettlement at Bibiani. A second contractor is clearing waste from a January pit-wall failure, with work targeted for completion in early December and renewed ore access in the first quarter.\nGroup output was 48,517 gold-equivalent oz. in the third quarter, at all-in sustaining costs of $4,860 per oz., Asante said. Its realized gold price averaged $4,178 per oz. over the period.\nThe withdrawn August forecast had envisaged 275,000–300,000 oz. of annual production at all-in sustaining costs of $3,200–$3,600 per ounce. At the time, the company said it had already deferred or cancelled about $50 million of planned capital expenditure.\nMine plan review\nThe Bridge drilling forms part of an 8,000-metre program intended to improve confidence in mineralization between existing pits. Asante wants to upgrade inferred resources and test whether material assigned to underground extraction can be incorporated into a revised open-pit design.\nBibiani lies about 80 km southwest of Kumasi. The operation is one of Asante’s two producing mines in Ghana, alongside Chirano.\nA broader operating turnaround plan is expected in January 2027, setting out corrective measures, milestones and capital needs, acting CEO Campbell Baird said in Friday’s results.\n“We continue to make progress in resetting the business, although there is still considerable work ahead of us,” Baird said. Print\nTAG: Ghana gold gold exploration gold mining gold production gold projects\nCOMMODITY: Gold\nCOMPANY: Asante Gold\nRepublish this article",
